# ADR-017: Move tool lifecycle from Coordinator to Tool

## Status

Accepted

## Context

The Coordinator carried per-tool logic in five methods:
_pre_execute_verify, _post_execute_verify, _update_state,
_compress_tool_result, _summarise_for_llm. Each method had a chain
of `if tool_name == X` branches, one per tool. Adding a new tool
required edits in all five places. The Coordinator knew the full
state schema, the verification rules per tool, the state-update
semantics, and the compression strategy of each tool. This is
classic shotgun surgery.

## Decision

Extend the Tool protocol with five lifecycle methods:
verify_before, execute, verify_after, update_state, compress_result.
A DefaultTool base class provides no-op defaults. Concrete tools
override only what they need.

The Coordinator's _execute_tool_call becomes a generic dispatch:
lookup, gate, verify_before, execute, verify_after, update_state,
compress_result, return ToolResult. No per-tool branches remain.
The terminal-status decision for escalate_to_human stays in the
Coordinator (flow control, not tool semantics).

## Consequences

Positive: Coordinator shrinks from 695 to 364 lines. Adding a new
tool no longer requires Coordinator edits. Each tool is self-
contained and unit-testable through its lifecycle methods. The 65
unit tests passed without any modification, confirming that the
public-API contract is preserved.

Negative: tool classes carry more code than before. The previous
"tools are dumb data accessors, the Coordinator orchestrates" model
is replaced by "tools are smart lifecycle units, the Coordinator
dispatches".

The _STATUS_MAP retains entries for now-unreachable consultable
rules. Cleanup followed in ADR-XXX (the _STATUS_MAP commit).

## Alternatives considered

A central VerificationOrchestrator class, separate from both the
Coordinator and the Tools, was considered. Rejected because it would
have moved the per-tool branching to a new location without
eliminating it. The Tool-Lifecycle approach genuinely co-locates
each tool's concerns with the tool itself.

A registry-based hook system (decorators that register verifications
against tool names) was considered. Rejected because it adds
indirection. Method overrides on a class are clearer for a reader.